✗ lifecycle hook exited without returning

  Hook "warren/broker/kafka" ended its goroutine during OnStop without
  returning and without panicking. The remaining hooks were stopped anyway —
  shutdown does not abandon the drain because one hook failed — and this is
  reported with whatever else failed on the way down. The resources this hook
  owns may not have been released.

  That is what runtime.Goexit does — and t.Fatal, t.Fatalf, t.FailNow and
  t.Skip are all runtime.Goexit. An assertion inside a lifecycle hook ends the
  hook's goroutine where it stands, and recover() sees nil on the way out, so
  Warren reports the exit and cannot report its cause: the message you wrote
  is already gone.

  Two ways to fix it:

    • Assert outside the hook. Let the hook record what it saw and return,
      then call t.Fatal from the test's own goroutine, where the message
      survives.
    • Return an error instead of failing inside the hook. A hook that returns
      an error is reported the same way, and the error reaches you intact.